\subsection{Complete-case sensitivity analysis}\label{sec:cc}
\input{tables/complete_case}
Restricting every dataset to images with all metadata recorded (Table~\ref{tab:cc}) removes label-informative missingness. Weak-versus-strong attenuation (H1) holds in \CCholdHone{} of \CCn{} datasets; within-deep attenuation (H2) in \CCholdHtwo{} of \CCn{}. On PAD-UFES-20, the standalone information in the metadata falls from $\PADIAleak\times10^{-3}$ to $\PADIAcc\times10^{-3}$ nats, suggesting that much of that dataset's apparent metadata signal reflects how missing values arise, although the complete cases are also a different population. Its retained fraction is nearly unchanged ($\rho=\CCRHOPAD$), because the leak inflated numerator and denominator alike, but the complete cases (\PADccKept{} images, mostly biopsied) form a different population. Complete-case retained fractions are \CCRHOHAM{} (HAM10000), \CCRHOISICtwenty{} (ISIC 2020), \CCRHOISICnineteen{} (ISIC 2019), and \CCRHOPAD{} (PAD-UFES-20).

\FINETUNESECTION

\subsection{Interpretability: where is the evidence?}
\input{tables/interp}
\input{tables/interp_backbones}
For each backbone we use the exact class activation map of the out-of-fold head. For ConvNeXt-T, whose pooled features pass through a LayerNorm before the head, the map is exact up to a positive per-image factor: with per-image LayerNorm scale $\sigma$ and affine weights $\gamma$, the logit equals a constant plus $\sigma^{-1}|\Omega|^{-1}\sum_u\sum_j \tilde w_j\big(F_j(u)-\bar F(u)\big)$ with $\tilde w_j=w_j\gamma_j/s_j$ and $\bar F(u)$ the channel mean. The overlap statistics are invariant to that factor, and the reconstructed logits matched the head's logits with correlation $1.000$ for every backbone.

Mapping the \emph{predicted} class's evidence produced an apparent link between errors and off-lesion attention ($p<10^{-49}$; Table~\ref{tab:interp}). This is an artefact: for ``not melanoma'' predictions, the evidence for that class lies in the surrounding skin. With the melanoma-evidence map used for every image (Table~\ref{tab:interpbb}), melanoma evidence is concentrated on the lesion in \INTbbEnrichRange{} of images across the three backbones. Missed melanomas show no less on-lesion evidence; instead they are consistently smaller lesions ($p\le\INTbbAreaPmax$ for every backbone). Pixel permutation declares \INTbbPermRange{} of maps significant, against \INTbbShiftRange{} under the exact shift null, consistent with the inflation predicted by its disregard of spatial autocorrelation~\cite{alexanderbloch2018,markello2022neuromaps}.

\section{Discussion and Limitations}
\textbf{What the results mean.} On every dataset, metadata add clearly more to a colour-histogram model than to frozen deep representations (H1 held in all three pre-registered replications). Attenuation \emph{within} deep representations is small and inconsistent: H2 held only on PAD-UFES-20. How much value remains at high capacity depends on the dataset rather than on the imaging modality (Table~\ref{tab:retained}). A ConvNeXt-T representation leaves $\rho=\RHOHAMCNX$ of the metadata's standalone information non-redundant on HAM10000 and $\rho=\RHOISICtwentyCNX$ on ISIC 2020, but $\rho=\RHOISICnineteenCNX$ on ISIC 2019 (non-HAM, largely BCN20000, which deliberately includes hard-to-diagnose sites~\cite{combalia2019bcn}) and $\rho=\RHOPADCNX$ on PAD-UFES-20; the complete-case values are similar (\CCRHOHAM{}, \CCRHOISICtwenty{}, \CCRHOISICnineteen{}, \CCRHOPAD{}; Table~\ref{tab:cc}). The frequently reported benefit of metadata~\cite{hohn2021review,pacheco2020impact} is therefore partly a statement about weak image representations, and the size of the part that is not differs severalfold between datasets.

\textbf{Practical implication.} Metadata fusion should be evaluated against the strongest available image model, and the retained fraction $\rho$ reported alongside the gain, so that a benefit that merely compensates for a weak backbone is not mistaken for complementary information.


\textbf{Limitations.} (i) Only frozen representations are compared along the ladder; fine-tuning is examined separately (Section~\ref{sec:ft}). (ii) $\hat\Delta$ estimates the reduction achievable by a logistic second stage, not $I(Y;A\mid S)$ itself. (iii) Capacity is ordered by image-only performance; the ladder is not a controlled intervention on a single architectural factor. (iv) The mechanism suggested by Proposition~\ref{prop:decomp} is not isolated: the pre-registered symptom test failed because symptoms are partly visible even within a diagnostic class. (v) ISIC and PAD-UFES-20 images come from public mirrors; official metadata were used, and counts and contents were checked against the published descriptions. ISIC 2019 images without a lesion identifier were grouped individually. (vi) Metadata missingness is label-informative in all four datasets, severely so in PAD-UFES-20. Complete-case analysis removes this leak but analyses a selected subsample. (vii) All analyses are retrospective and internal (cross-validated); nothing here is a clinical claim. (viii) Saliency overlap is evidence of spatial correspondence, not of causal reliance~\cite{adebayo2018sanity}.

\section{Conclusion}
The value of patient metadata to a skin lesion classifier is not fixed: it depends on what the image model already extracts. Proposition~\ref{prop:decomp} makes this precise. Grouped, pre-registered experiments show that metadata add far less to deep representations than to colour histograms on HAM10000, and this weak-versus-strong attenuation replicated in all three independent datasets. Attenuation among deep representations did not replicate consistently, and the share of metadata information left non-redundant by a strong representation ranged severalfold across datasets. Claims that metadata improve a classifier should therefore be made against strong image baselines and accompanied by the retained fraction, not a gain over a weak model.

